{
  "_doc": "The goals in three tiers (story/goals.gd; design spec section 10), shown beside the story card (ui/goal_readout.gd). One goal of each tier is active at a time. tiers[]: id and name (the stencilled label on the HUD row), in the order the rows are listed, and patience_s (optional): an authored goal that holds the tier's row this many seconds of play with no progress steps aside for good, so a goal the player is not after never blocks the row (it still pays when it is finished); the first tier is the short one, and its active goal is what the story card carries once the story steps are done and no source job is on offer. short = the next concrete thing (minutes), medium = a city milestone (a mastery level, a city's stage, its source job, a crew size), long = the planet's arc (the launch point, the Sprout set, the share of the whole planet). goals[]: id (never changes once shipped: saves keep the ids of finished goals), tier, text (short, starts with a verb, must fit the readout row), type and key (what is counted: story/goal_measures.gd lists every type), target, reward. A tier's active goal is its first goal in this order that is on offer and not done; a goal with after_flag comes before the rest of its tier. A goal is on offer when its after_flag (a save flag) is set and its `when` condition holds; `when` is {type, key, target} with the same measures as a goal (cities_open n = the n-th city has opened; upgrade baler = the Baler is fitted). So each city's goals wait for the city, and a city's last stage is listed after the next city's third stage, because the crew finishes it while the player has moved on. repeat: a repeating goal, the tier's fallback when no authored goal is on offer, so a tier is never empty: it asks for `repeat` more of stat `key` than when it started counting (text holds %d), plus repeat_grow each time it was finished, up to repeat_max. Once a tier has fallen back on them, all its repeating goals on offer count side by side and pay as they are finished, and the row shows the one nearest done, so the next small win is always the one on show. guide (optional): where the beam points for this goal, {to: bench|depot|bin|hex|source, row: [workbench tab, row id], district}; without it the goal's type decides (story/goal_guide.gd): bench rows for upgrades, robots, rigs and builds, the scale for selling, the source's spot for a source job, the outlined dirty hex for everything done on the ground. reward: paid the moment the goal's condition holds, wherever the goal stands in its tier (never held back for the card, never in a burst). A reward is in the currency of the action that earned it and small (design spec section 14: never more than about 3 minutes of income): cubes for cleaning, selling, earning, upgrading and learning a kind by hand; seeds for planting and regrowth; a Part for digging parts up; nothing but the Lumi XP for crew, rig, find and Locker goals (Parts come from salvage alone).",
